drills.explanation:
Decide the page size, ask for one row more, and use the surplus only as a signal:

```js
const size = pageSize(params.limit);            // whole number, default 20, clamped to 1..100
const rows = await db.query({ after: params.after ?? null, take: size + 1 });
const hasMore = rows.length > size;
const items = hasMore ? rows.slice(0, size) : rows;
const last = items[items.length - 1];
return { items, hasMore, next: hasMore ? { createdAt: last.createdAt, id: last.id } : null };
```

The probe row costs one extra row from an index that is already being read; a count costs a scan of everything. `next` is built from the last row you RETURN, not from the probe row, or the following page would skip one.

**What the tests catch.** The plausible wrong answer fetches exactly one page and sets `hasMore` when the page came back full: when the table holds exactly one full page it promises a next page that is empty. Calling `db.count()` fails the query-log assertion, an unclamped limit asks the database for 100,001 rows, and returning the probe row hands the client eleven items.
